Date: Sat, 1 Aug 2026 23:40:55 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08107/11362] The re-ask can turn a success into a hard
 failure, and 17 of 21 treatment cells did

Pinned as a regression test, plus the artifact builder that reports it. The measured live result:
induction HARD-FAILED on 17 of 21 treatment cells against 1 of 22 control and 0 of 21 A/A, 11 of
the first 16 on the focused goal-only call -- the one call shape carrying both halves of the
intervention.

`generate()` claims a defect re-ask "NEVER FAILS WHERE THE OLD PATH SUCCEEDED". It is false.
`attempt < tries - 1` stops the LAST attempt from continuing out of the loop; it does not stop an
EARLIER re-ask from spending the attempt that would have BEEN the accept. Identical scripted
replies -- a usable-but-defective answer on attempt 0, then two malformed completions -- give
accept with the flag off and hard failure with it on. The test fails the moment someone decouples
the retry ladder, which is when the comment becomes true and should be restored.

The same structure is in the SHIPPED engine gate, so its 13/36 -> 22/36 was obtained under this
trade and its cost in hard failures has never been measured.

The pre-registered primary is therefore NOT an estimate of anything: the surviving treatment cells
are the subset whose re-asked answers happened to parse. The builder emits it labelled as such,
and names the regression in the verdict rather than reporting a readable null.

The matching comment correction in arc_executable_world_model.py and the artifact JSON are held
back: artifact-freshness-lint refuses every commit touching that module or any results/*.json
because 7 registered artifacts are stale against it, which HEAD already was before this session
touched anything. Rebuilding them means rewriting artifacts authored elsewhere while a concurrent
session edits the same module. Reported, not worked around; --no-verify was never used.

